@@ -549,3 +550,73 @@ Schemas themselves are never filtered out, since the remaining objects need thei
 For DDL replication, a single statement can create objects of several types at once (`CREATE TABLE` with a primary key produces both a table and an index). Such an event is only skipped when *all* of its objects belong to excluded categories.
 
 > ⚠️ Filtering does not resolve dependencies between object types. Excluding a category that surviving objects depend on (for example excluding `types` while keeping tables with columns of those types) will make the snapshot or the DDL replay fail. Use this with a good understanding of your schema.
+
+
+### Transaction-framed webhooks
+
+`source.postgres.replication.plugin.include_transactions: true` (or
+`PGSTREAM_POSTGRES_REPLICATION_PLUGIN_INCLUDE_TRANSACTIONS=true`) preserves
+wal2json begin (`B`) and commit (`C`) events. This opt-in mode requires a strict,
+replication-only webhook target without row modifiers. Other configurations are
+refused before streaming. Source-level `add_tables` and `filter_tables` remain
+available.
+
+Subscribe once with empty schema/table and event types `B`, `C`, `M`, alongside
+the required row subscriptions. In unsliced mode, the selected receiver sees every
+exposed event exactly once before it can validate a transaction. A begin has
+`transaction_index: 0`; subsequent events have consecutive indices starting at
+one. A commit has index `N+1`, `transaction_count: N`, and native `nextlsn`. The
+count excludes suppressed internal pgstream DML and includes logical messages.
+Distinct rows can share an LSN; use the transaction index to distinguish them.
+
+Begin and commit also carry `source_identity` with `system_id`, `timeline`,
+`database`, and `slot`, read through PostgreSQL `IDENTIFY_SYSTEM` and refreshed
+on reconnect. This source identity is separate from an application's restore
+generation. Neither XID nor an LSN alone is a source identity.
+
+The connector checkpoints only a commit's `nextlsn`, after successful delivery
+of that commit. Accepted rows and begins never advance the checkpoint. Idle
+keepalive responses repeat the last acknowledged position; they do not advance
+to the server's observed WAL end. Restart therefore replays an incomplete
+transaction from its begin. Receivers must durably retain accepted partial
+input before returning success and publish only validated complete transactions.
+
+Without `target.webhooks.notifier.transaction_batch`, the connector keeps
+constant-size framing state. The optional transaction batch buffers one complete
+cohort under explicit `max_rows` and `max_bytes` bounds before any HTTP delivery.
+Receiver bounds, recoverable overflow, and atomic application publication remain
+receiver responsibilities. Empty filtered transactions may
+produce begin/commit pairs; a receiver must avoid writing receipt state for an
+empty transaction if those writes are in the same source database. Such writes
+would otherwise create an input feedback loop.
+
+#### Static tenant slices for transaction webhooks
+
+The optional `target.webhooks.notifier.transaction_batch.slice_receivers` is an ordered
+array of distinct private `http://host:port/pgstream` instance URLs (1–1024 entries).
+Index zero is ordinal zero. Every routed table and B/C/M subscription must cover
+exactly this set. Subscription query order does not change the ordinal ordering.
+The receiver count and order are immutable until every old owner has stopped.
+
+The producer validates the complete original native transaction before splitting it.
+For each I/U/D row, the lower-case canonical `tenant_id` UUID selects the receiver:
+SHA-256 over UTF-8, unsigned first eight digest bytes in big-endian order, modulo the
+receiver count (`sha256-modulo-v1`). Missing, malformed, duplicate or changing tenant
+identities refuse the original transaction. A native `pgstream.ddl` M marker reaches
+every receiver; unsupported control markers and tenantless T events refuse.
+
+A receiver gets `{"Slice":{"count":K,"ordinal":i},"Transaction":[B,...,C]}`.
+B and C are unchanged, including their source identity and commit position. DML rows
+only reach their selected ordinal. M markers retain their order in every projection.
+Original transaction indices retain gaps and C retains the original complete count;
+receivers must explicitly validate this sliced contract. Empty slices receive no
+B/C-only callback. An empty original cohort keeps the existing no-webhook checkpoint.
+
+`max_rows` and `max_bytes` still bound the original cohort. Each projected body must
+fit `max_bytes`, including the Slice envelope; aggregate projected bodies must fit
+the existing sender queue budget. Projection adds no checkpoint or delivery queue.
+Strict mode advances the shared source position only after every selected receiver
+accepts its body. A refused or uncertain delivery stops the producer; restarting
+replays the original position and identical projected bodies. A failed receiver can
+therefore pause later intake on healthy slices until platform replacement and
+producer restart/reconnect complete. This option does not introduce peer takeover.
